"""English source copy: explain decisions and interactions, not field lists."""
APPEARANCE = "Choose an icon set that fits your workspace. The optional collision overlay reveals differences between the visible model and the geometry used for contact; showing it does not change whether bodies collide."
SIMULATION = "These defaults apply at the next start to bodies without their own overrides. Gravity and damping determine the load an actuator must overcome: more damping settles oscillations but also resists intended motion. Add the ground at Z = 0 only when you need it, since it can catch parts that should pass below the model."
QUALITY = "Use Quick while setting up a large mechanism, then check demanding contacts with Precise. This changes how closely constraints are solved, so small differences in contact or joint motion are possible; the geometry and cable resolution stay the same."
RUNTIME = "Newton runs in a separate Python environment containing MuJoCo. If that environment is moved or removed, both simulation and collision inspection stop working; select its new Python executable here. A working Runtime needs no changes when you edit the model."
PROFILES = "A profile copies parameter values onto a body while preserving its placement and reference bodies. Bodies remember their profile, but keep local values until you explicitly sync them. Sync replaces profile parameters, including local edits; use the sidebar for one body or queue all linked bodies here for OK. Built-ins are read-only, but you can inspect their values and duplicate one as your own starting point."
BODY = "The collision shape determines where a body can actually move. A hull that closes an opening can block a mechanism even when the visible parts clear each other; child components keep the gaps between parts open. Check that geometry before increasing motor force, because mass and friction add further load."
MOTION = "A servo aims for a position, while a motor aims for a speed. Speed and acceleration shape that request, but available force, loads and contacts determine what the body achieves. Limits stop travel, and stronger braking helps stop before them; sync braking with acceleration when the same response is wanted in both directions."
CONNECTION = "Use a connection to close a mechanism, such as the link between a connecting rod and a slider, without changing its reference-body hierarchy. Its permitted motion must agree with the axes already present: adding a fixed connection can lock the mechanism. Place or drag its marker in the model to change the attachment point."
FLEXIBLE = "The line defines a bendable strand that keeps its length. Increasing its radius makes it much harder to bend and also leaves less clearance inside a guide, so adjust radius and stiffness together. Damping settles the strand, while a clamped start also resists rotation; stiff, finely segmented strands need more computation."
COLLISIONS = "Exclude contact between parts that are already guided by a joint, such as a pin inside its bearing, when their overlap would otherwise jam the mechanism. The pair can then pass through each other, so the joint must provide the restraint. Contacts with other bodies remain active."
OVERRIDES = "Use overrides when this body needs a different environment. It still collides with and pulls on connected neighbours, so local gravity can move an entire mechanism. Switch overrides off to inherit the global defaults again; a zero gravity vector instead explicitly makes this body weightless."
FIELDS = "Combine attraction with a sideways starting velocity to create an orbit instead of a straight fall. Attraction grows near the centre; a larger softening radius keeps close passes from producing extreme forces. Initial velocity is applied only when starting a freely movable body."
COUPLING_FREE = "Couple axes when one control should move several parts, such as two gripper jaws. The follower's limits and speed also restrict the shared movement, so a tight setting on either side can stop both."
COUPLING_FOLLOWER = "This axis shares the leader's control instead of using its own drive. Direction and ratio set its relative movement, while either axis's limits can restrict the shared travel. The leading actuator must supply force for both bodies."
COUPLING_LEADER = "{bodies} follow this control. Their additional load and limits also affect the leading axis; select a follower to change how far or in which direction it moves."
PROFILE_OVERRIDES = "An enabled override replaces the global environment when this profile is applied. It can change the load on connected parts as well as the selected body; leave it off when the profile should follow the simulation's defaults."
